## basically OOP has 4 things:
- Class → blueprint
- Object → real thing from blueprint
- Method → function inside class
- Attribute → variable inside class

## simple example i tried:

class Dog:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    def bark(self):
        print(f"{self.name} says woof!")

dog1 = Dog("Bruno")
dog1.bark()  # Bruno says woof!

## inheritance (this was cool)
class Animal:
    def breathe(self):
        print("breathing...")

class Cat(Animal):  # Cat gets Animal methods
    def meow(self):
        print("meow!")

cat = Cat()
cat.breathe()  # works! inherited!
cat.meow()

## what i understood today:
→ class is like a template
→ self means "this object"
→ __init__ runs automatically
→ child class gets parent methods

